      { q: 'What is "conching" in chocolate making?', opts: ['Removing cacao husks', 'Long agitation of chocolate mass to develop flavor and texture', 'Tempering chocolate over ice', 'Grinding cacao nibs into liquor'], answer: 1, fact: 'Conching — invented by Rodolphe Lindt in 1879 — involves continuously agitating melted chocolate for hours to days in a conche machine. This drives off acetic acid and other volatile off-flavors, coats particles in cocoa butter for smoothness, and rounds the flavor profile.' },
      { q: 'Which compound in chocolate is primarily responsible for the mood-lifting effect?', opts: ['Caffeine', 'Theobromine', 'Phenylethylamine', 'Anandamide'], answer: 1, fact: 'Theobromine — a mild xanthine alkaloid related to caffeine — is the primary active stimulant in chocolate. It provides a gentler, longer-lasting lift than caffeine. Theobromine is also why chocolate is toxic to dogs and cats, whose livers metabolize it far more slowly than humans.' },
      { q: 'What is the purpose of "tempering" chocolate?', opts: ['Adding sugar to reduce bitterness', 'Heating and cooling to form stable cocoa butter crystals', 'Removing moisture to extend shelf life', 'Emulsifying milk solids evenly'], answer: 1, fact: 'Cocoa butter can form six different crystal structures. Tempering guides it to form Type V (Form 5) crystals — which give chocolate its glossy sheen, satisfying snap, and smooth melt. Untempered chocolate blooms grey and crumbles.' },
