      { q: 'White chocolate contains no cocoa solids. What gives it its flavor?', opts: ['Vanilla and sugar only', 'Cocoa butter, milk solids, sugar, and vanilla', 'Defatted cocoa powder', 'Caramelized milk'], answer: 1, fact: 'White chocolate contains cocoa butter (the fat extracted from cacao), milk solids, sugar, and vanilla — but zero cocoa solids (the dark, flavored part). Some countries\' regulations require at least 20% cocoa butter content for it to be labeled white chocolate.' },
      { q: 'The Maillard reaction and caramelization during roasting of cacao beans creates what?', opts: ['Fermentation of sugars', 'Development of hundreds of flavor and aroma compounds', 'Removal of bitter compounds', 'Formation of theobromine'], answer: 1, fact: 'Cacao roasting is where most of the complex flavor development occurs. The Maillard reaction (amino acids + reducing sugars) and caramelization at 120–150°C create over 600 volatile flavor compounds — from fruity and floral to nutty and earthy. Roast level is one of the most critical decisions a chocolate maker makes.' },
      { q: 'Which Swiss city is considered the birthplace of milk chocolate?', opts: ['Zurich', 'Geneva', 'Vevey', 'Basel'], answer: 2, fact: 'Daniel Peter of Vevey, Switzerland, created the first successful milk chocolate in 1875 by using Henri Nestlé\'s condensed milk to solve the moisture problem that caused chocolate-milk mixtures to seize. The partnership between Peter and Nestlé would reshape the global confectionery industry.' },
      { q: 'What is the "flavor precursor" created during cacao fermentation that is essential to chocolate taste?', opts: ['Free fatty acids', 'Reduced amino acids and sugars', 'Tannins', 'Sucrose'], answer: 1, fact: 'During 5–7 days of fermentation, yeast and bacteria break down pulp sugars and proteins. The heat and pH changes inside the bean kill the seed and trigger enzymatic reactions that create free amino acids and reducing sugars — the Maillard precursors that become flavor during roasting. Without proper fermentation, even perfect roasting can\'t create good chocolate.' },
