      { q: 'What is the French culinary technique "mise en place"?', opts: ['Blanching vegetables before cooking', 'Everything in its place — prepping all ingredients before cooking begins', 'Resting meat after roasting', 'Building a roux base for sauces'], answer: 1, fact: '"Mise en place" — "put in place" — is the foundational practice of measuring, chopping, and organizing all ingredients before cooking begins. It\'s not just a prep method; it\'s a philosophy of discipline, efficiency, and respect for the craft that defines professional kitchen culture worldwide.' },
      { q: 'What is a "roux" and what is its primary function?', opts: ['A garnish made from fried herbs', 'Equal parts fat and flour cooked together to thicken sauces', 'A clarified butter technique', 'A slow-cooked stock reduction'], answer: 1, fact: 'A roux is equal parts (by weight) fat and flour cooked together. The starch granules absorb the fat and swell when liquid is added, thickening the sauce. The color and flavor depend on cook time — white roux for béchamel, blonde for velouté, dark for gumbo and Cajun cooking.' },
      { q: 'What technique is used to make Hollandaise sauce?', opts: ['Cold emulsion with a whisk', 'Warm emulsion of egg yolks and clarified butter over gentle heat', 'Reduction of cream with butter', 'Binding with cornstarch'], answer: 1, fact: 'Hollandaise is a warm emulsion — egg yolks whisked with a reduction of white wine and vinegar over a bain-marie (double boiler), then clarified butter added drop by drop. The lecithin in egg yolks acts as the emulsifier. Temperature control is everything: too hot and the eggs scramble, too cool and the butter won\'t emulsify.' },
      { q: 'What is "fond" in classical French cooking?', opts: ['A cold appetizer course', 'Caramelized browned bits left in the pan after searing', 'A chilled sauce base', 'A dry spice mixture'], answer: 1, fact: '"Fond" (French for "base" or "foundation") refers to the caramelized proteins and sugars stuck to the bottom of a pan after searing. Deglazing with wine, stock, or vinegar dissolves the fond in a process called "déglacage" — the starting point for an enormous range of pan sauces and braises.' },
      { q: 'Confit is a preservation technique that involves cooking meat in what?', opts: ['Salt brine', 'Its own fat at low temperature', 'Vinegar and sugar', 'Vacuum-sealed water bath'], answer: 1, fact: 'Confit (from the French "confire," to preserve) involves slow-cooking meat — traditionally duck, goose, or pork — submerged in its own fat at 80–90°C for several hours. The cooked meat stored under fat was a pre-refrigeration preservation method. Duck confit legs are one of the defining preparations of Gascon cuisine.' },
