      { q: 'Alabama white sauce — a regional BBQ specialty — is made primarily from:', opts: ['Mustard and honey', 'Mayonnaise, apple cider vinegar, and horseradish', 'Cream and butter', 'Vinegar and pepper only'], answer: 1, fact: 'Alabama white sauce was invented by Robert "Big Bob" Gibson in Decatur, Alabama in the 1920s. Made from mayonnaise, apple cider vinegar, black pepper, and horseradish, it\'s used as both a finishing sauce and a dipping sauce, primarily for smoked chicken. Its tangy, creamy profile is unique in the BBQ world.' },
      { q: 'What is the stall (or "Texas crutch" phenomenon) in long BBQ cooks?', opts: ['A period when the smoker runs out of fuel', 'A temperature plateau at ~150–170°F caused by evaporative cooling', 'Over-rendering of fat that pools in the cook', 'A resting period mandated for food safety'], answer: 1, fact: 'The "stall" or "plateau" occurs when large cuts of meat — typically brisket or pork shoulder — reach an internal temperature of around 150–170°F (65–77°C) and stay there for hours. Evaporative cooling from moisture moving to the surface counteracts the heat input. The "Texas crutch" is wrapping the meat in butcher paper or foil to power through it.' },
