      { q: 'What creates the reddish-brown "crema" on top of a well-pulled espresso?', opts: ['Added cream or milk proteins', 'Emulsified coffee oils and CO₂ gas under pressure', 'Surface caramelization of sugars', 'The Maillard reaction in the cup'], answer: 1, fact: 'Crema is formed when high-pressure water forces CO₂ (trapped in freshly roasted coffee cells) into solution. As it exits the portafilter, it nucleates into micro-bubbles that emulsify with coffee oils, forming the stable foam layer. Fresh beans produce more crema; stale beans produce little to none.' },
      { q: 'A "ristretto" shot differs from a standard espresso in what way?', opts: ['It uses more coffee grounds', 'It is extracted with less water over a shorter time — more concentrated', 'It is pulled with lower pressure', 'It uses a coarser grind'], answer: 1, fact: 'Ristretto ("restricted" in Italian) uses the same coffee dose but stops the extraction early — typically 15–20ml instead of 25–30ml. The shorter contact time extracts more of the sweeter, brighter early-extraction compounds and less of the bitter late-extraction compounds, producing a sweeter, more intense, lower-volume shot.' },
