      { q: 'What does it mean for coffee to be "single origin"?', opts: ['Grown on a single farm or micro-lot from one traceable source', 'Roasted by a single roaster', 'A blend of one arabica and one robusta variety', 'Certified organic from one country'], answer: 0, fact: 'Single origin coffee comes from one traceable source — a specific country, region, farm, cooperative, or even lot. It allows roasters and drinkers to taste the terroir: the influence of soil, altitude, climate, and processing on flavor. The contrast is with blends, which combine multiple origins for consistency.' },
      { q: 'What is the ideal water temperature range for espresso extraction?', opts: ['75–80°C / 167–176°F', '85–95°C / 185–203°F', '98–100°C / 208–212°F', '70–75°C / 158–167°F'], answer: 1, fact: 'Water for espresso should be 90–96°C (194–205°F) — just below boiling. Too hot extracts bitter, harsh compounds; too cool under-extracts, leaving the shot sour and thin. Darker roasts are often brewed slightly cooler; lighter, denser specialty roasts may need hotter water for complete extraction.' },
      { q: 'The "third wave" of coffee movement emphasizes which core values?', opts: ['Maximum caffeine output and speed', 'Artisanal sourcing, traceability, light roasting, and coffee as craft', 'Espresso-only service', 'Robusta-forward blending for strength'], answer: 1, fact: 'Third wave coffee — emerging in the early 2000s with roasters like Stumptown, Intelligentsia, and Counter Culture — treats coffee as an artisanal product, emphasizing farmer relationships, single origins, light roasting to preserve origin character, and precise brewing. It reacted against both commodity coffee (first wave) and chain-brand espresso dominance (second wave).' },
